    "q-214198": {
      "dom_id": "step-q214198",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given the arithmetic sequence \n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}-6,\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,n\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nthe index of the term $-124$ is $n=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We will convert the recursive formula to an explicit formula and then solve for the value of $n$ such that ${a}_{n}=-124.$\n\nThe recursive rule for an arithmetic sequence takes the form\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+d.\n$$\n\nIn the given formula, we have $d=-6.$ We are also told that ${a}_{1}=2,$ so we can write the explicit formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =2+(n-1)(-6) \\\\  & =2-6n+6 \\\\  & =-6n+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ such that ${a}_{n}=-124:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-6n+8 \\\\ -124 & =-6n+8 \\\\ -132 & =-6n \\\\ \\frac{-132}{-6} & =n \\\\ 22 & =n \\\\ n & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-124$ is the $22$nd term of the sequence.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
