      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Given an arithmetic sequence that has ${a}_{3}=21$ and ${a}_{5}=27,$ what is the index of the term $108?$",
      "worked_solution": "To construct the formula for the sequence, we need to know the common difference $d$ and the first term ${a}_{1}.$\n\nSince we are given ${a}_{3}=21$ and ${a}_{5}=27,$ we can find the common difference by dividing the total difference by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{5}-{a}_{3}}{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27-21}{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{3}=21$ is $2$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+2d \\\\ 21 & ={a}_{1}+2(3) \\\\ 21 & ={a}_{1}+6 \\\\ 15 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =15+(n-1)(3) \\\\  & =15+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=108,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =3n+12 \\\\ 108 & =3n+12 \\\\ 96 & =3n \\\\ \\frac{96}{3} & =n \\\\ 32 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $108$ is the $32$nd term of the sequence.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
