    "q-214239": {
      "dom_id": "step-q214239",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "If an arithmetic sequence has ${a}_{6}=12$ and ${a}_{9}=24$, find the value of $n$ such that ${a}_{n}=132.$\n\n$n=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To construct the formula for the sequence, we need to know the common difference $d$ and the first term ${a}_{1}.$\n\nSince we are given ${a}_{6}=12$ and ${a}_{9}=24,$ we can find the common difference by dividing the total difference by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{9}-{a}_{6}}{9-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{24-12}{9-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{3} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{6}=12$ is $5$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}+5d \\\\ 12 & ={a}_{1}+5(4) \\\\ 12 & ={a}_{1}+20 \\\\ -8 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-8+(n-1)(4) \\\\  & =-8+4n-4 \\\\  & =4n-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=132,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =4n-12 \\\\ 132 & =4n-12 \\\\ 144 & =4n \\\\ n & =\\frac{144}{4} \\\\ n & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $132$ is the $36$th term of the sequence.",
