    "q-5397": {
      "dom_id": "step-q5397",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Given an arithmetic sequence that has ${a}_{2}=5$ and ${a}_{5}=-4$, what is the index of the term $-73?$",
      "worked_solution": "To construct the formula for the sequence, we need to know the common difference $d$ and the first term ${a}_{1}.$\n\nSince we are given ${a}_{2}=5$ and ${a}_{5}=-4,$ we can find the common difference by dividing the total difference by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{5}-{a}_{2}}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-5}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9}{3} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{2}=5$ is $1$ jump after ${a}_{1},$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+d \\\\ 5 & ={a}_{1}-3 \\\\ 8 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =8+(n-1)(-3) \\\\  & =8-3n+3 \\\\  & =11-3n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=-73,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =11-3n \\\\ -73 & =11-3n \\\\ -84 & =-3n \\\\ \\frac{-84}{-3} & =n \\\\ 28 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-73$ is the $28$th term of the sequence.",
